    "narrative": "For LawDog on May 29, 2026, the engine room of our lineup centers on a favorable environment for hitters facing the likes of Lorenzen and Samaniego. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** leads the charge with a 74.98 score, buoyed by a prime batting order position and a favorable matchup against the lefty Samaniego. He is flanked by two primary beneficiaries of the hitter-friendly park factor: **Matt Chapman** (69.74) and **Luis Arraez** (69.53). Both players see significant bumps from the park factor and an exploitable matchup against Lorenzen. Meanwhile, **Jonathan Aranda** (66.11) remains a high-floor start today, as the dynamic platoon advantage against Ure\u00f1a effectively neutralizes the concerns surrounding the opponent's xERA.\n\nThe decision-making process was tightest regarding **Bryson Stott** (42.91), who was the first player off the active roster. While **Bryson Stott** offers some upside against a slower pitcher\u2014a key factor for our emphasis on stolen bases\u2014the matchup against Wrobleski created a negative platoon drain that pushed his efficiency below the threshold of our current starters. Elsewhere, we are leaning on **Taylor Ward** (59.22) at the top of the order; despite the challenging 2.80 xERA of Macko, **Taylor Ward**\u2019s leadoff status and dynamic platoon edge make him a critical component of our daily scoring floor. We continue to monitor the status of **Cal Raleigh** and **Yainer Diaz**, both of whom remain sidelined on the IL, leaving our catching depth thinner than we would prefer.\n\nRisk management remains a priority today, as **Will Smith** (43.58) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (45.64) are slotted into the lineup despite facing high-level arms in Wheeler and Kirby. These starts are necessitated by our game-cap management strategy, but we are acknowledging the low efficiency floor created by these difficult pitching matchups. **Daulton Varsho** (55.49) is a watch-item as well; while his park factor is excellent, the heavy 